// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row replace_site_skips_field_drop_glue (tier 1). A
// DROP-BEFORE-REPLACE RUNS THE USER `Drop::drop` AND NOT THE VALUE'S OWN FIELD
// DROP GLUE, SO EVERY OWNING FIELD OF THE REPLACED VALUE IS LEAKED WHILE THE
// DESTRUCTOR COUNT STAYS EXACTLY RIGHT.
//
// Measured 2026-09-08 on build 8e5f92705b285d29 43, by re-running the valgrind
// sweep of tests/logos/unrowed_backlog.ledger entry `vg_leak_records` against
// today's compiler. THE SHAPE SWEEP, one binary, one control per line
// (sandbox/vg-round2/hand/shapes.logos), destructor count CORRECT on every one:
//
//   local, scope exit        `let a = mk();`                      CLEAN
//   moved into a callee      `consume(a)`                         CLEAN
//   if-expression arms       `let k = if c { a } else { b };`      CLEAN
//   ASSIGNMENT over a local  `let mut k = mk(); k = mk();`         64 bytes LOST
//   ASSIGNMENT over a field  `h.p = mk();`                        128 bytes LOST
//   struct FIELD at scope ex `let h = Holder { p: mk() };`         64 bytes LOST
//
// AND THE OBJECT FILE SAYS WHY, per emission site (objdump of that one binary):
//   s_scope   1 x Pay__drop  1 x Vec__drop      <- correct
//   s_ifexpr  3 x Pay__drop  3 x Vec__drop      <- correct
//   s_assign  2 x Pay__drop  1 x Vec__drop      <- one field glue missing
//   s_field   2 x Pay__drop  0 x Vec__drop      <- both missing
//   s_nested  1 x Pay__drop  0 x Vec__drop      <- missing
// So the recursion into a value's fields after its user `Drop::drop` is emitted
// at ONE site (a top-level local at scope exit) and at no other. ⚠ Whether the
// two failing sites are one root or two is NOT MEASURED: this round did not
// touch the compiler, so nothing proves one candidate change moves both
// (rule 13 — a per-site measurement is not additive).
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS IS INVISIBLE TO EVERY ORACLE IN THE TREE. The user destructor runs
// exactly once per value, so the DESTRUCTOR-COUNT oracle — the strongest one the
// corpus has, and the one `cond_move_*`, `rawdup_*`, `dupown_*` are built on —
// is GREEN. The exit code is 0 and stdout is right. Only valgrind sees it.
// 32 of the 90 leaking fixtures leak through `vec_new` reached from their own
// `mk`, which is this shape; how many of those 32 this root accounts for is
// NOT measured and must not be claimed.
//
// THIS PROGRAM makes the same defect visible to an exit code by giving the
// replaced value a field whose own destructor is observable: two `Pay` values
// are created and both destroyed, so two `Noisy` destructor calls are owed.
// MEASURED: c[0] == 1, rc 11. A correct compiler exits 0.
// Rust: both `Noisy` drops run; `let _ = &mut k;` is not needed — assignment
// over an initialised place drops the old value COMPLETELY (RFC 320 drop glue).
package replace_site_skips_field_drop_glue;
extern fn malloc(n: i64) -> *mut u8;

trait Drop { fn drop(self: Self); }

struct Noisy { n: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for Noisy { fn drop(self: Noisy) { unsafe { self.c[0] = self.c[0] + 1i64; } } }

struct Pay { n: i64, inner: Noisy }
impl Drop for Pay { fn drop(self: Pay) { } }

fn mk(n: i64, c: *mut i64) -> Pay { return Pay { n: n, inner: Noisy { n: n, c: c } }; }

unsafe fn main() -> i32 {
    let buf: *mut u8 = malloc(8);
    let c: *mut i64 = buf as *mut i64;
    c[0] = 0i64;
    {
        let mut k: Pay = mk(1i64, c);
        k = mk(2i64, c);            // drop-before-replace: old k's Noisy is owed a drop
    }
    // two Pay values created, both destroyed => two Noisy destructor calls owed
    if c[0] != 2i64 { return (10i64 + c[0]) as i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
